NAME
       ssh_config - settings of the ssh client

SYNOPSIS
       ~/.ssh/config
       /etc/ssh/ssh_config
       /etc/ssh/ssh_config.d/*.conf

DESCRIPTION
       ssh, scp and ssh-copy-id read their settings from the command line
       (-o), then from your own ~/.ssh/config, then from the system file
       /etc/ssh/ssh_config (which includes /etc/ssh/ssh_config.d/*.conf).
       For each keyword the first value found wins, so your own file beats
       the system one.

       The file is split into blocks by Host lines. The settings under
       "Host PATTERN" apply when the name you typed matches the pattern
       (* matches anything). Put specific hosts first and "Host *" last.

       ~/.ssh/config must not be writable by others, or ssh refuses it.

KEYWORDS
       Host PATTERN...
              Start a block for these host names.

       HostName NAME
              The real host name or address to connect to (so a short alias
              can stand for a long name).

       User NAME
              The remote user name.

       Port N The remote port.

       IdentityFile FILE
              The private key to use.

       StrictHostKeyChecking yes|no|accept-new|ask
              What to do with an unknown host key: ask (default), refuse,
              accept-new (save new keys without asking, still refuse changed
              ones) or no.

       UserKnownHostsFile FILE
              Where to keep known host keys.

       PasswordAuthentication yes|no, PubkeyAuthentication yes|no
              Which login methods to try.

       BatchMode yes|no
              Never ask questions (for scripts).

       LogLevel QUIET|ERROR|INFO|VERBOSE
              How much to print.

EXAMPLES
       Make "ssh s2" log in to server2 as student with a particular key:

           Host s2
               HostName server2.lab.example.com
               User student
               IdentityFile ~/.ssh/lab_key

SEE ALSO
       ssh(1), sshd_config(5), ssh-keygen(1)
